PRE-TABLE AMENDMENT, PASS 8 -- its own file (mtime against part8/driver.log is the order witness).

SECTION 16 -- the VALUE DOMAIN of each body-read lvalue, and the rows that hold its extremes.
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
Pass 7 restated the enumeration per arm and held each lvalue at ONE non-initial value.  Pass 8
(the kimi-k3:cloud substitute, packet-fed; deepseek) showed that a guard predicate true of the
initial value AND of that one value passes every row:
* TxCTL's overflow count: `ovf*' hold it at 1 (bit 4).  `&& !(reg & 0xe0)' passes all 171.
  Reachable by a guest re-arming the count each loop iteration (one overflow per host callback,
  verified by hand on /tmp/gxpcc2b7b: 0x81 after ~80 ms).  Rows `ovfhi2 *' / `ovfhi1 *' (count 8).
* the pending counts: `pend*' hold them at 1.  `&& pending < 2' passes all 171.  Reachable: COC
  with compare 1 -- one callback adds thousands (pcc_timer_tick:231-236; verified by hand).
  Rows `pendn2 *' / `pendn1 *'.
* the value 0 was driven at SCCERR and T1ICR only -> `latch all zero' (twelve 0x0000 writes).
* TxICR bit 3 ('by presets' was FALSE: the body's `& 0x17' skips it) and bits 6-7, and TxCTL
  bits 2-3 (deepseek): set by NO model writer (TxCTL 2-3 only via the filed pcc2counter8 raw
  copy) -> E, written into the listing with the writer census.
* the timer presets 0x03 / 0x13 carry bit 1 (a packet gap, not a cell); 'fourteen guarded
  targets' was a miscount (twelve); the warn-latch at the T1 labels is the shared block bit.
* grok (file-reading): session()'s (cmd, wait, until) tuple was unpacked BEFORE its third element
  was read, so `until' became the command's third character -- present in the buffer at once --
  and the wait after `continue' returned immediately.  The pass-7 claim "session() now stops
  waiting when the BREAKPOINT text arrives (20 s ceiling)" was FALSE as implemented (L4); the
  rows passed because the queued commands are consumed after the breakpoint anyway.  Fixed: the
  tuple is read before it is unpacked.  Part 8's BASE is the first run in which the wait works.
* glm (packet): under M_W4FALL_ALL the GENCTL rb4/rb8 rows are that mutant's KILLING rows and were
  MODIFIED (lanes 0xd9/0x40) after its last measurement, so "additions cannot un-kill" does not
  reach them: the by-value GENCTL kill was UNMEASURED.  M_W4FALL_ALL is re-measured in part 8.
STOP CONDITION for this class: a lvalue's domain is stated as the values rows hold; a predicate
true of ALL of them is DISCLOSED, not owed -- the reachable extremes (count 8, pending in the
thousands, value 0/1/3 at every arm) are held, and nothing enumerates every integer.
* codex (file-reading): TxICR bit 2 -- an interrupt-level bit -- is clear in the 0x13 and 0x31
  presets, a legal byte write 0x14 sets it, and `&& !(reg & 0x04)' at the TxICR guard passes
  every row: in closure (a bit the byte read holds, guest-reachable).  Rows `rb t2icr il4' /
  `rb t1icr il4' (preset 0x17).  Also: the companion decl/rb rows of a free-running session
  graded a never-reached phase (M_IDATA_NE1_ALL.out: `pend2 arm = no-breakpoint' beside a green
  `pend2 decl') -> a missed token now INVALIDATES the session and every companion row says so
  (cval); the residual unbounded waitpid after a failed kill is bounded.  TxCTL bits 2-3 via the
  counter memcpy: reachable through the filed pcc2counter8 raw copy only; no row is built on a
  defect's path on purpose (stated in the contract).  TxCTL reads relative_addr too (listed).
* grok (file-reading): the `until' clobber (above); dead bits (COVF, TxCTL bit 3, TxICR bit 3 and
  6-7) named as dead rather than 'full bit-domain'; `idata != 2' is the disclosed value-test
  shape; the sgi_de probe's session() still has the unbounded waitpid (a different round's file).
* opus (file-reading, running notes): F2 a SIGKILLED guest reported how = "exit0" -- WNOHANG
  returns (0, 0) while the child lives, so `stat' was 0 not None, the killed label was
  overwritten by WIFEXITED(0), and the killed child was never reaped -> the reap is restructured
  (reaped flag; kill; bounded reap; the label survives).  F4 "seven arms read nothing": all
  read `idata' (the value under test) -> reworded.  F5 the IPL arm's READ branch has a second
  static -> named, outside the closure, held anyway by `ipl rset'.  F7 the pass-8 gate finished
  0.39 s BEFORE the last pass-7 probe edit, so no gate has run against the shipped probe (its
  rows were measured by part 7b's BASE; its 171 name checks were not) -> the part-8 gate runs on
  the final file.  Its own extra point on F1: the inert wait CUT the guest's time to reach its
  breakpoint from 8 s to ~0 s, so the pass-7 "repair" made the load-sensitivity worse.
* opus (running notes, later): the IPL arm's READ-branch static (`ipl rset' holds it -- the
  seat's zero-session form, prepending a byte read to `ipl wset', was not taken so the two
  statics' kills stay distinguishable); the CROSS-BIT latch cell -- a guard at arm A qualified
  by arm B's bit is killed by `latch all' only when B precedes A, so GENCTL (first) was covered
  by nothing -> `latch all rev' (the twelve writes in reverse order; with the forward row every
  ordered pair is covered); the closure asymmetry it raised (OVF nibble in, reg[IPL] out) is
  consistent under the rule as written -- what the guarded WRITE body READS decides, whoever
  writes the state -- and the contract says so.
* opus (final report): its own two mutants on the shipped 171-row instrument -- M_IPLR (the
  READ-branch static hoisted into the IPL guard) 171/171 and M_WARNX (GENCTL's guard naming
  MASK's latch bit) 171/171, both predicted before the build -- are the cells `ipl rset' and
  `latch all rev' close.  Its third finding supersedes the bits-2-3 disclosure: the counter
  arm's raw memcpy at T2COUNT (0x10) is 8-ALIGNED, so a guest st.d reaches it and lands
  arbitrary bytes on T2CTL/T1CTL -- TxCTL bits 2-7 are guest-reachable through the filed
  pcc2counter8 path, and one stepping session holds them: `rb t2ctl b27' / `rb t1ctl b27'
  (put d 0x...fcfc at T2COUNT, then the wide write; declined, 0xfc unchanged).  The rows say in
  their own comment that they rest on a filed defect and must be re-cut when it is fixed.
  Also: the pass-7 as-run drivers carry a stale pass-2 header comment (documentation only);
  pcc2counter8's filed text swaps the T1CTL/T2CTL labels (corrected in OUTSTANDING_BUGS).
PREDICTIONS for part 8 (eight mutants plus BASE, 190-row instrument; BASE is the first run in
which the breakpoint wait actually operates):
    BASE               190/190
    M_TXCTL_OVFHI      186/190   ovfhi2 decl, ovfhi2 rb (0x80), ovfhi1 decl, ovfhi1 rb (0x80);
                                 the count-1 `ovf*' rows stay green (0x11 & 0xe0 == 0); the b27
                                 rows too (0xfc & 0xe0 != 0 -> the guard declines them)
    M_PEND2_LT2        188/190   pendn2 decl, pendn2 rb (0x20); pend2 (count 1) and every pendn1
                                 row green
    M_IDATA_NE0_QUAL   186/190   latch all zero, wwr sccerr dcl, pend1 decl, pend1 rb (0x20)
    M_W4FALL_ALL       164/190   rb4 x11 and rb8 x11 (the nine storing blocks + t1ctl + t1icr),
                                 pscale noig4/8, ipl noig4/8 -- 26 red; rb4/rb8 genctl red BY
                                 VALUE (readback 0xd9 / 0x40, host alive), no longer by death
    M_TXICR_IL4        188/190   rb t2icr il4 (0x10), rb t1icr il4 (0x10); every 0x13-preset row
                                 and the pend rows (0x31: bit 2 clear) stay green
    M_WARNX_GENCTL     189/190   latch all rev (11: MASK is written first, its bit set, and the
                                 GENCTL write lands); the forward `latch all' stays green
    M_IPLR             189/190   ipl rset (DECL 0: the byte read of IPL at init sets the read
                                 branch's latch, then the wide write lands) -- the Opus seat
                                 measured this mutant at 171/171 on the shipped instrument
    M_TXCTL_COVF       188/190   rb t2ctl b27 (0xff), rb t1ctl b27 (0xff): the landed 0x0303 keeps
                                 bits 2-7 and sets bits 0-1; every other TxCTL row has bit 2 clear

ADDENDUM (written before the table that counts; the first launch of part 8 is preserved as
part8_aborted/): the first launch scored BASE 188/190 -- `rb t2ctl b27' and `rb t1ctl b27' read
0x0 because the rows were built without setup(), so the guest's base register was unset and the
readback hit RAM, not the device (the hand-verified mini-probe had set the register itself).  The
driver only WARNED and went on to its first mutant -- the tablebasenotfull class in this round's
own template -- so the run was stopped at its first mutant, the two rows now run setup() first,
and the driver aborts on a non-full BASE.  Part 8 is relaunched on the corrected instrument; the
predictions above are unchanged.
